The 30-Year Fixed Mortgage Rate is the most common type of U.S. home loan. As its name suggests,
it takes borrowers 30 years to pay back the entire loan, and the interest rate is fixed at the
then-current mortgage rate when the contract is signed. The mortgage rate has risen from 
2.74% in January 2021 to 6.86% in September 2026. By the time this blog post is written, the national
average is approximately 7.40% to 7.43%. 

The causes of the high level of
mortgage rate are intricate, as they involve several layers of transmission, each layer
represents stakeholders that are pertinent to the rates. Starting with the 
Federal Funds Rate. The Fed raises this rate in order to curb inflation, and the housing
market is the main channel of monetary policy. This ultra short-term
rate then directly affects the yields on 10-year Treasury, which is the benchmark of mortgage rates. 
Higher mortgage rates means home purchases are less affordable, and this matters
to all potential homebuyers.
According to Atlanta Fed, the median monthly
payment of home purchase takes about 27.8% of a median household income in January 2021. Now it takes
more than 45%, effectively deterring many buyers. 

[WRITE, paragraph 2: why it matters, concretely: what it does to a monthly payment on a typical
loan; why the Fed watches it (housing is the main channel of monetary policy); why investors who
hold mortgage credit price it as a spread over Treasuries. Then the question: how much of the
rise is the Treasury yield, how much is the spread, and within the Treasury yield, is it expected
real rates, expected inflation, or risk premia?]

## Two decompositions

The benchmark for mortgage rate is the 10-year Treasury yield. That is, mortgage rate tends
to closely follow the 10-year Treasury (as opposed to the Federal Funds rate). The difference
between the 30-year mortgage rate and the 10-year Treasury consists of two spreads: 
(i) secondary spread: prepayment risk, credit risk, and liquidity, 
(ii) primary-secondary spread: service fee, net guarantee fee, etc.
This post will treat the two spreads as a whole and the equation is simply 
$\text{mortgage rate} = \text{10-year Treasury yield} + text{
mortgage–Treasury spread}$. This is not only for simplicity, but more importantly, as we will
discuss later in the post, the spread is not the main driver of the higher rates in the current period. Our focus is
on the decomposition of the 10-year Treasury yield. The Cleveland Fed's term-structure model breaks the 10-year 
Treasury yield into four components: expected path of real short rates, expected inflation, real risk premium,
and inflation risk premium. The equation below gives a clearer representation of everything put together:

$$
\underbrace{i^{mort}_t}_{\text{mortgage rate}}
= \underbrace{\big(\bar r^{e}_t + \pi^{e}_t\big)}_{\text{expected policy path}}
+ \underbrace{\big(\rho^{real}_t + \rho^{\pi}_t\big)}_{\text{risk premia}}
+ \varepsilon_t + s_t
$$
where
- $\bar r^{e}_t$: expected real interest rate
- $\pi^{e}_t$: expected inflation rate
- $\rho^{real}_t$: real risk premium
- $\rho^{\pi}_t$: inflation risk premium
- $varepsilon_t$: Cleveland Fed's model residual
- $s_t$: mortgage-Treasury spread 

Then the data choices: monthly averages of all series, January 2021 as the base (the mortgage
trough, which maximizes the measured change by construction), the model residual kept as its
own piece so the pieces add up exactly.]


## The spread's history

![](fig1_levels.png)
Figure 1 gives a simple decomposition of the 30-year mortgage rate. The spread's long-run norm is
1.69 pp on average from 2010 to 19, and it peaked at 2.97 pp in June 2023. It is now printing at 1.87 pp,
which is just slightly above the long-run average pre-pandemic and post-GFC.

## Decomposition and share of contribution

![](fig2_contributions.png)
Figure 2 shows the complete decomposition of the mortgage rate. The blue bar indicates expected real
short rates, which is the market's expectation of future path of monetary policy. It has been the main
driver of mortgage rates and 10-year Treasury for the majority of the sample period. We could see that even at 
the October 2023 peak, the spread was only a quarter of the story, and inflation expectation and 
expected real rates are predominantly leading. 

| | Jan 2021 → Oct 2023 (pp) | % | Jan 2021 → Sep 2026 (pp) | % | Jan → Sep 2026 (pp) | % |
|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| Expected real short rates | 2.03 | 41 | 2.31 | 56 | 0.49 | 65 |
| Expected inflation | 0.98 | 20 | 1.19 | 29 | 0.24 | 32 |
| Real risk premium | 0.29 | 6 | 0.17 | 4 | 0.09 | 11 |
| Inflation risk premium | 0.07 | 1 | 0.07 | 2 | 0.01 | 1 |
| Model residual | 0.35 | 7 | 0.17 | 4 | -0.05 | -7 |
| Mortgage-Treasury spread | 1.17 | 24 | 0.22 | 5 | -0.02 | -2 |
| Total: 30-year mortgage rate | 4.88 | 100 | 4.13 | 100 | 0.76 | 100 |

: Contributions to the change in the 30-year mortgage rate, percentage points and share of the total change. Shares can be negative or exceed 100 when pieces offset. Source: FRED (Freddie Mac PMMS, Federal Reserve Board H.15, Federal Reserve Bank of Cleveland); author's calculations. {#tbl-contrib}

To get a more concrete sense of what's driving the changes in mortgage rates, we could look
at the share of contribution by components in three different periods, shown in the table above.
The period from January 2021 to October 2023 saw rates from trough to peak, the main driver
being the market's expectation of the short-term policy rate (41%). The spread comes next
at 24%. But if we look at a longer window from January 2021 to September 2026, the contribution
of mortgage-Treasury spread shrinks to 5%. What's more pertinent to the scope of this blog post
is the year-to-date contribution. Since the beginning of 2026 until now, expected real short
rates and expected inflation together accounts for 97% of the increase in mortgage rates. 

To put those data into context
fiscal concern - debt-to-GDP ratio, higher interest payment -> higher real risk premium, but only explains part of the story
higher energy prices - inflation risk premium, almost negligible at 1%
AI-related debt issuing - not necessarily reflected in the model
resilient economic growth - expected inflation

## Cleveland Fed's model vs. NY Fed's model

[WRITE: the Cleveland split is one model's answer. Compare with the NY Fed's ACM model and with
TIPS. Three points: (1) both models say the expected policy path, not the term premium, is the
main driver, and in 2026 both say all of it; (2) it is a real-rate story, TIPS says almost
entirely real; (3) the models disagree on the term premium, 0.23 vs 1.31 pp, so state it as a
range. One sentence on why Cleveland's expected inflation rose more than breakevens: its model had
10-year expected inflation at 1.38% in January 2021 against a 2.08% breakeven.]

| | Jan 2021 → Oct 2023 | Jan 2021 → Sep 2026 | Jan → Sep 2026 |
|---|---:|---:|---:|
| Cleveland: expected nominal path (real path + expected inflation) | 3.01 | 3.51 | 0.73 |
| Cleveland: premia (real + inflation risk premium) | 0.36 | 0.23 | 0.09 |
| ACM: expected nominal path | 2.87 | 2.57 | 0.72 |
| ACM: term premium | 0.91 | 1.31 | -0.02 |
| TIPS: real yield | 3.41 | 3.64 | 0.73 |
| TIPS: breakeven inflation | 0.30 | 0.27 | 0.04 |
| 10-year Treasury yield (actual) | 3.72 | 3.91 | 0.78 |

: Change in the 10-year Treasury yield and its parts under two models and in the TIPS market, percentage points. Source: FRED, Federal Reserve Bank of Cleveland, Federal Reserve Bank of New York (ACM); author's calculations. {#tbl-models}

## What moves the spread

![](fig3_spread_drivers.png)

[WRITE: read Figure 3. The spread rose with rate volatility (prepayment option) and as the Fed
stopped buying and then let MBS run off; it fell back as volatility fell even though the Fed is
still shrinking its holdings. Say plainly that this is co-movement, not a causal estimate.]

## Caveats

- The components for the decomposition are model estimates (Haubrich–Pennacchi–Ritchken), and a different model splits
  them differently (see @tbl-models) and we could potentially arrive at different conclusions. But in this case, different
  models at least agree with each other about the main driver.
- the residual: mean −0.33 pp over the sample, up to 1.23 pp in absolute value; it moved 0.17 pp
  over the window;
- the base is the trough, which maximizes the measured change;
- the spread is PMMS minus the 10-year constant-maturity yield, not an MBS option-adjusted spread;

## Takeaway

To conclude, of the 4.13 pp rise in mortgage rates since January 2021, 3.91 is the Treasury yield and 0.22 is
the spread. Within the Treasury yield, the expected path of real policy rates accounts for 2.31 pp
(56% of the mortgage move) and expected inflation for 1.19. 


risk premia are small under the
Cleveland model and a third of the move under ACM. The spread problem of 2023 has largely gone;
the rate problem has not.]

## Data and code

- **Mortgage rate:** Freddie Mac Primary Mortgage Market Survey, 30-year fixed, via FRED (`MORTGAGE30US`), weekly
- **Treasury yields:** Federal Reserve Board H.15, via FRED: 10-year constant maturity (`DGS10`), 10-year TIPS (`DFII10`), 10-year breakeven (`T10YIE`), daily.
- **Fed MBS holdings:** Federal Reserve Board H.4.1, via FRED (`WSHOMCB`), weekly.
- **Yield decomposition:** Federal Reserve Bank of Cleveland, [Inflation Expectations](https://www.clevelandfed.org/indicators-and-data/inflation-expectations), via FRED: `EXPINF10YR`, `REAINTRATREARAT10Y`, `TENEXPCHAREARISPRE`, `TENEXPCHAINFRISPRE`, monthly.
- **Term premium cross-check:** Federal Reserve Bank of New York, [Treasury Term Premia](https://www.newyorkfed.org/research/data_indicators/term-premia-tabs) (Adrian, Crump and Moench), daily file.
- **Code:** [github.com/1iiiiii/Mortgage-Rate-Decomposition](https://github.com/1iiiiii/Mortgage-Rate-Decomposition). 
